{"candidates":[{"content":"The registry-confounding account in SRC_005 abstracts SRC_003's one-repository arbitrariness claim by specifying the causal mechanism through which arbitrary installation history generates the 3-of-26 distribution: the registry's enumeration semantics are defined by the test's own installation history, so the test constrains only entries installed under a detectable guard. Refuted if the 3 surviving mutations are re-run under ordinary installation order and are not caught, or if the registry has deterministic enumeration order and the survivors do not cluster in a way consistent with installation-history-defined semantics.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"007dca563e8ddb537138b6410d65570617eb77284afbd4f0318b06d9f0fe45cf"},{"role":"dependence","target":"0b35ed752e77f1579edf2b3bd734eb0aaefa3a3dbd2a5c84bc84d9b305fbbe63"}],"typicality":0.72},{"content":"SRC_001's registry-size account reduces to SRC_004's order-effect explanation as a special case: the mutation count per module determines whether a given guard installation order can produce a measurable selective effect, so the observed asymmetry is a function of both order and size, not a general testing condition. Refuted if ordinarily-guarded modules each had dozens of mutations and still lost 4/4 to 6/7, or if mutation proposal and guard installation order were randomized across the project and the asymmetry persisted.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"c00c0237298149a2527c41416fd9b3a2cbe136f98b2724189687d7179132ee2d"},{"role":"dependence","target":"bccf5ac4d334e048e6aa4face1992604c3d42be375c0c5ffd166d7ec209e0285"}],"typicality":0.65},{"content":"SRC_005 shares mechanism with SRC_002 in that both explain the 1-of-9 compile.py loss as an installation-discipline failure rather than a stable property of tests; SRC_005 specifies the mechanism as a coverage gap in a directly tested code path, while SRC_002 frames it as a content-edit hole, but both deny generalizability from the record. Refuted if the 1-of-9 compile.py mutation is shown to be in a directly tested code path and the coverage-gap explanation is thereby confirmed against SRC_002's content-edit framing, or if the pattern replicates across authors and weeks.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"007dca563e8ddb537138b6410d65570617eb77284afbd4f0318b06d9f0fe45cf"},{"role":"dependence","target":"fa18c80e19d07923c18d905c4ea3b215e743388eb9709f921579b6ec5c114570"}],"typicality":0.58},{"content":"SRC_004 integrates SRC_003's arbitrariness claim by adding temporal ordering as the operative variable: the 3-of-26 result is an artifact of mutation proposal order and guard installation order in one repository, which is the specific temporal mechanism behind SRC_003's one-author, one-week confound. Refuted if a formal policy mandated guard-before-mutation ordering and compile.py still showed 1/9, or if the same distribution replicated with a different author following a different order.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"bccf5ac4d334e048e6aa4face1992604c3d42be375c0c5ffd166d7ec209e0285"},{"role":"dependence","target":"0b35ed752e77f1579edf2b3bd734eb0aaefa3a3dbd2a5c84bc84d9b305fbbe63"}],"typicality":0.69},{"content":"SRC_002 contradicts SRC_001's implicit commitment to a positive mechanism (registry-size confound) by asserting that the evidence is too small and contaminated to support any transferable condition at all: SRC_001 proposes that mutation count explains the distribution, while SRC_002 claims the 3-of-26 result warrants no mechanism beyond local artefact. Refuted if the 4/4 to 6/7 consistency in ordinarily-guarded modules survives replication across a different repository, author, and week, thereby establishing a regularity that SRC_002 denies is inferable.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"fa18c80e19d07923c18d905c4ea3b215e743388eb9709f921579b6ec5c114570"},{"role":"dependence","target":"c00c0237298149a2527c41416fd9b3a2cbe136f98b2724189687d7179132ee2d"}],"typicality":0.41},{"content":"SRC_005 is compatible with SRC_004: both explain the 3-of-26 result as an installation-order artifact, but SRC_005's registry-semantics mechanism (the test constrains only entries installed under a guard the test can detect) is consistent with SRC_004's sequential guard-installation account, and neither requires a generalizable testing condition. Refuted if the same shown-to-fail ritual on a larger, multi-author repository reproduces the 3-of-26 ratio, or if the mutation proposal and guard installation order were randomized and the distribution did not show the observed asymmetry.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"007dca563e8ddb537138b6410d65570617eb77284afbd4f0318b06d9f0fe45cf"},{"role":"dependence","target":"bccf5ac4d334e048e6aa4face1992604c3d42be375c0c5ffd166d7ec209e0285"}],"typicality":0.74}],"simulation_proposals":[]}